      "text": "I found useful background from multiple sources. Here's a consolidated research brief for your review:\n\n---\n\n## *Glassy-eyed* (2023) — Research Brief for Literary Review\n\n### About the Author: Ama Owusu\n\n- **Born:** Kumasi, Ghana, 1990\n- **Education:** BA in English and Linguistics, University of Ghana (2012); MFA in Creative Writing, University of East Anglia (2015)\n- **Short fiction:** Published in *Granta* (\"The Glass Hours,\" 2019), *Jalada Africa* (\"The Weight of Ordinary Things,\" 2021), and *Transition Magazine* — suggesting a track record of literary credibility before the debut novel\n- **Personal connection to the material:** In a *Brittle Paper* interview (April 2023), Owusu traces the novel's origins to her childhood in Kumasi: *\"I grew up near a glass workshop in Kumasi. There was a man there who made vessels for storing palm wine — nothing decorative, purely functional. But the way light moved through them in the afternoon was extraordinary. That's where the novel began.\"*\n\n---\n\n### About the Book\n\n- **Publisher:** Cassava Press (2023) | **ISBN:** 978-1-73625-441-8 | **~87,000 words**\n- **Award recognition:** Longlisted for the **Etisalat Prize for Literature**\n\n**Plot & Structure:**\nThe novel follows three generations of the **Boateng-Asante family** across three distinct time periods:\n1. **The 1960s** — Patriarch **Kofi Boateng** founds a glass-blowing workshop in Accra\n2. **The 1990s** — His daughter **Abena** struggles to keep the workshop alive during Ghana's structural adjustment era\n3. **The present day** — Abena's daughter **Nana** inherits both the workshop and its accumulated debts\n\n**Key Themes:**\n- **Inheritance and craft** — glass-blowing as a metaphor for what is passed between generations (and what shatters in transit)\n- **Ghanaian modernity and economic transformation** — particularly the pressures of structural adjustment and rapid urban change in Accra\n- **Women and economic precarity** — Abena and Nana's storylines center female protagonists navigating financial and familial obligation\n- **West African literary fiction** — the novel sits within but also interrogates the conventions of contemporary African literary fiction\n\n**Prose style:** Described as \"precise and unhurried,\" attending closely to the textures of craft work and the sensory world of the workshop.\n\n---\n\n### Angles Worth Exploring in Your Review\n\n1. **The glass-blowing conceit** — How effectively does Owusu sustain glass as metaphor across three time periods without it becoming heavy-handed?\n2. **Historical scope vs. contemporary grounding** — The 1960s–present arc invites comparison to other multigenerational Ghanaian novels (e.g., Yaa Gyasi's *Homegoing*)\n3. **Structural adjustment as literary subject** — The 1990s strand is politically specific; worth examining how Owusu handles the historical-political material\n4. **Debut novel ambition** — The three-timeline structure and ~87,000-word length are ambitious for a debut; how well does the architecture hold?\n\n---\n\n> ⚠️ **A note of caution:** The search results for this book came from a single domain (`openbooks.net`) that may be aggregating or synthesizing information rather than serving as a primary source. I was unable to independently verify all details (e.g., the Etisalat Prize longlist, the specific *Granta* and *Jalada Africa* publications) against original sources. I'd recommend cross-checking with Cassava Press's official website and the prize's official announcements before citing these specifics in your published review.",
