nomic precarity** — Abena and Nana's storylines center female protagonists navigating financial and familial obligation\n- **West African literary fiction** — the novel sits within but also interrogates the conventions of contemporary African literary fiction\n\n**Prose style:** Described as \"precise and unhurried,\" attending closely to the textures of craft work and the sensory world of the workshop.\n\n---\n\n### Angles Worth Exploring in Your Review\n\n1. **The glass-blowing conceit** — How effectively does Owusu sustain glass as metaphor across three time periods without it becoming heavy-handed?\n2. **Historical scope vs. contemporary grounding** — The 1960s–present arc invites comparison to other multigenerational Ghanaian novels (e.g., Yaa Gyasi's *Homegoing*)\n3. **Structural adjustment as literary subject** — The 1990s strand is politically specific; worth examining how Owusu handles the historical-political material\n4. **Debut novel ambition** — The three-timeline structure and ~87,000-word length are ambitious for a debut; how well does the architecture hold?\n\n---\n\n> ⚠️ **A note of caution:** The search results for this book came from a single domain (`openbooks.net`) that may be aggregating or synthesizing information rather than serving as a primary source. I was unable to independently verify all details (e.g., the Etisalat Prize longlist, the specific *Granta* and *Jalada Africa* publications) against original sources. I'd recommend cross-checking with Cassava Press's official website and the prize's official announcements before citing these specifics in your published review.",
